From jl.verissimo-Re5JQEeQqe8AvxtiuMwx3w at public.gmane.org  Thu Oct 16 23:30:11 2014
From: jl.verissimo-Re5JQEeQqe8AvxtiuMwx3w at public.gmane.org (=?utf-8?q?Jo=C3=A3o_Ver=C3=ADssimo?=)
Date: Fri, 17 Oct 2014 01:30:11 +0200
Subject: Re: How to drop non-linear terms for ordered factors in lmer?
In-Reply-To: <CAPJVwBksVojrjNSoOCZ_poR+ST_mBC3jpmJOww+YZ5-uPKYsYg@mail.gmail.com>
References: <CAFrHNueFgNEWRpXL5UvxhkuW5NxsghTg9pJ6c_32xamx1PFnzA@mail.gmail.com> <CAPJVwBksVojrjNSoOCZ_poR+ST_mBC3jpmJOww+YZ5-uPKYsYg@mail.gmail.com>
Message-ID: <1413502211.28320.18.camel@zeca>

Alternatively, because polynomial contrasts are the default for ordered
factors, you could change the contrasts of the factor (for example, to
treatment contrasts):
contrasts(repetition) <- contr.treatment(4)

Or "repetition" could still be treated as a factor, but unordered (in
which case treatment contrasts would be applied by default):
repetition <- factor(repetition, ordered=F)

When making "repetition" numeric, and given that it enters into an
interaction with context, I would center it.

Hope this helps,
João

